    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2052062386329113021",
      "url": "https://x.com/AidenReports/status/2052062386329113021",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/AidenReports/status/2052062386329113021",
      "text": "🚨 🇯🇵 🇵🇭 🇺🇸\nHistoric moment: Japanese troops fire on foreign territory for the first time since World War II\n\nDuring the massive Balikatan 2026 military exercise, about 1,400 Japanese Ground Self-Defense Force troops deployed to the Philippines and fired Type 88 anti-ship missiles from Ilocos Norte. \n\nThey successfully sank the decommissioned Philippine ship BRP Quezon, roughly 50–75 km offshore.\n\nThis marks the first combat use of the Japanese missile system outside Japan’s territory since WWII.\n\nThe drill involving the US, Philippines, Australia, and others (total 17,000 troops from 7 nations) simulates defending against an amphibious assault in the South China Sea. It runs from April 20 to May 8.\n\nChina has strongly condemned the exercise as “remilitarization” and “playing with fire.”\n\nSource: Official military statements",
